{"term": "chienne", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "bitch_insult", "concept": "bitch_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 68, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 98, "recognition_pct_peak": 98, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'female dog'; used as an insult for a contemptible or mean woman; the standard French equivalent of 'bitch'.", "english_term": "bitch", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Can also carry sexually voracious connotations in erotic contexts ('une chienne au lit' = a wildly uninhibited sexual partner). The literal animal term gives it slightly different texture from 'salope'."}
{"term": "garce", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "bitch_insult", "concept": "bitch_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 65, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 95, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A mean, wicked, or sexually loose woman; functions as both 'bitch' and a mild 'slut' accusation depending on context.", "english_term": "bitch / hussy", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Historically meant 'female vagabond' or 'loose woman'; in contemporary use primarily means a nasty or wicked woman, with lingering sexual-looseness connotation. Slightly milder and more literary than 'salope'."}
{"term": "traînée", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 78, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A promiscuous woman; literally 'something dragged along the ground'; a strongly pejorative slur for a woman perceived as sexually indiscriminate.", "english_term": "slut / tramp", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "From 'traîner' (to drag/trail); implies a woman who drags herself from man to man. One of the most common French slut-shaming terms, used across age groups."}
{"term": "pouffiasse", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 85, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A vulgar, trashy, sexually permissive woman; a very crude compound insult combining vulgarity and promiscuity.", "english_term": "slag / slut", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Etymology disputed; possibly 'pouf' (fart/poof) + augmentative suffix '-asse'. Implies both vulgarity/ugliness and sexual looseness. Among the harsher female-directed insults in French."}
{"term": "grognasse", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "bitch_insult", "concept": "bitch_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 70, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A disagreeable, ugly, or bad-tempered woman; from 'grogner' (to grunt/grumble); implies a coarse, unpleasant female.", "english_term": "hag / old bag", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primarily evokes unpleasantness and ugliness rather than promiscuity, though used broadly as a general female insult. Less sexually charged than 'salope' or 'traînée'. The '-asse' suffix adds contempt."}
{"term": "gourgandine", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "promiscuous_woman", "concept": "promiscuous_woman_slur", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 38, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 42, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "An archaic French term for a wanton, promiscuous, or licentious woman; a strumpet or hussy.", "english_term": "strumpet / hussy", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Attested from the 16th–17th centuries; today used only in literary, historical, or deliberately archaizing contexts. Virtually unknown among speakers under 40. Sometimes used ironically or affectionately in contemporary comedy."}
{"term": "cocu", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "cuckoldry", "concept": "cuckoldry", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 45, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 97, "recognition_pct_peak": 97, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A man whose wife or partner is unfaithful; a cuckold; one of the most culturally embedded figures in French comedy and literature.", "english_term": "cuckold", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "From 'coucou' (cuckoo), which lays eggs in other birds' nests. Deeply embedded in French farce and vaudeville tradition. Less politically loaded than English internet-slang 'cuck'; primarily refers to the domestic situation rather than political weakness."}
{"term": "cornard", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "cuckoldry", "concept": "cuckoldry", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 60, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A vulgar synonym for cocu (cuckold); from 'corne' (horn), referencing the traditional pan-European 'horns of the cuckold' symbolism.", "english_term": "cuckold", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "More contemptuous and cruder than 'cocu'. The cuckold's metaphorical horns are pan-European (Italian 'cornuto', Spanish 'cornudo'); the French 'corne' tradition is equally ancient."}
{"term": "baiseur", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "fucker", "concept": "fucker_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 72, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 87, "recognition_pct_peak": 87, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'one who fucks'; describes a sexually active or promiscuous man, used neutrally, laudatorily, or as an insult depending on context.", "english_term": "fucker", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From 'baiser' (to fuck; archaically, to kiss). Unlike the equivalent applied to women, 'baiseur' can carry mild admiration for virility in male-coded contexts. In the insults register it implies contemptible aggressiveness."}
{"term": "baiseuse", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 78, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 79, "recognition_pct_peak": 79, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Feminine form of 'baiseur'; a woman perceived as sexually promiscuous; subject to sharply stronger social condemnation than its male counterpart.", "english_term": "slut", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The gendered asymmetry is stark: 'baiseur' (man who fucks) can be mildly complimentary; 'baiseuse' (woman who fucks) is almost exclusively pejorative, illustrating the French sexual double standard."}
{"term": "salopard", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "scumbag", "concept": "scumbag", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 75, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 96, "recognition_pct_peak": 96, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A bastard, scoundrel, or despicable person; masculine form derived from 'salope' (dirty/slutty woman) + pejorative augmentative '-ard'.", "english_term": "bastard / scumbag", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Despite its feminine etymological root ('salope'), 'salopard' is used almost exclusively for men and has largely shed its sexual-specific meaning. One of the most common strong general-purpose French insults."}
{"term": "enculé", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "sodomy_insult", "concept": "cocksucker_insult", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 95, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'one who has been sodomized'; used as an all-purpose extremely strong insult, equivalent in force to 'motherfucker'; one of the harshest terms in French.", "english_term": "motherfucker / fucker", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Past participle of 'enculer' (to sodomize). In actual use it is a general intensifier and insult rather than a literal accusation. Originally carried heavy homophobic connotations; these persist but have partially eroded in general profanity use."}
{"term": "ordure", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "scumbag", "concept": "scumbag", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 68, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally filth, excrement, or refuse; used as a strong insult for a morally despicable or contemptible person.", "english_term": "scumbag / filth", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Literally means excrement or household refuse. As a person-insult it frames the target as human garbage. Also used in the plural ('ordures') for a group. No specifically sexual connotation, but appears in the insults-built-from-sexual-vocabulary section because the root connects to bodily disgust."}
{"term": "raclure", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "insults", "subcategory": "scumbag", "concept": "scumbag", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 65, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 84, "recognition_pct_peak": 84, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'a scraping' or 'residue'; the lowest, most contemptible sort of person; the dregs of humanity.", "english_term": "scum / dregs", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From 'racler' (to scrape). Implies the person is the residue scraped off the bottom of the barrel. Often intensified as 'raclure de bidet' (bidet scraping), adding a sanitary/sexual humiliation dimension."}
{"term": "prostitution", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "sex_work", "concept": "sex_work", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 10, "technical": 70, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The practice of engaging in sexual activity in exchange for payment; the standard clinical and legal French term.", "english_term": "prostitution", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Direct Latin-derived cognate with English. Carries social stigma in context but is not itself a vulgar word. France adopted a 'Nordic model' abolitionist law in 2016 (criminalizing clients, not sex workers)."}
{"term": "proxénétisme", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "procuring", "concept": "procuring", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 15, "technical": 88, "recognition_pct_current": 76, "recognition_pct_peak": 76, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The formal legal and clinical term for the crime of procuring or pimping; any act of organizing, benefiting from, or facilitating the prostitution of another.", "english_term": "procuring / pimping", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "The charge used in French criminal law. Includes both active pimping and passive exploitation (living off a sex worker's earnings). Lower recognition than 'prostitution' because it is primarily a legal/administrative term."}
{"term": "proxénète", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "pimp", "concept": "pimp", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 20, "technical": 75, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A pimp or procurer; the person who manages and profits from the prostitution of others; the formal legal and neutral term in French.", "english_term": "pimp / procurer", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "From Greek 'proxenetes' (go-between/broker). The clinical/legal term preferred in journalism and court contexts, as distinct from the slang 'maquereau'. Used for both male and female pimps."}
{"term": "maison close", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "brothel", "concept": "brothel", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 18, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": 1940, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'closed house'; the standard French term for a licensed brothel, referring to the regulated establishments abolished by the Marthe Richard law in 1946.", "english_term": "brothel", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "'Closed' referred to the shuttered exterior facade (discreet) rather than restricted access. Abolished in France 1946. Still widely recognized through literature, film, and historical discussion; periodically debated in French public policy."}
{"term": "maison de tolérance", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "brothel", "concept": "brothel", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 10, "technical": 50, "recognition_pct_current": 60, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": 1900, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'house of tolerance'; the official 19th-century French administrative euphemism for a state-regulated brothel under the Napoleonic regulatory system.", "english_term": "licensed brothel / house of tolerance", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Reflects the French réglementation policy (Napoleon–1946) of tolerating rather than eliminating prostitution through licensed supervision. More historical/academic than 'maison close'; lower recognition among speakers under 50."}
{"term": "racolage", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "soliciting", "concept": "soliciting", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 25, "technical": 65, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 87, "peak_year": 2003, "regional": null, "definition": "The act of soliciting clients for sexual services in a public place; the French legal term for street solicitation, criminalized by the 2003 Sarkozy law.", "english_term": "soliciting / streetwalking", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Criminalized under the Loi pour la sécurité intérieure (2003), which controversially made 'racolage passif' (passive solicitation via dress or presence) an offense. Decriminalized in 2016. The 2003 controversy drove recognition to its peak."}
{"term": "prostituée", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "sex_worker", "concept": "sex_worker", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 32, "technical": 40, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A prostitute; a person, typically a woman, who engages in sexual activity in exchange for money; the standard common-usage French term.", "english_term": "prostitute", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Standard term, neither strongly clinical nor slang. Sex worker advocates prefer 'travailleuse du sexe' as a neutral alternative. Listed in the slang/crude section of this dataset but functions as everyday vocabulary across all registers."}
{"term": "maquereau", "lang": "fr", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "sex_work_pornography", "subcategory": "pimp", "concept": "pimp", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 58, "technical": 0, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Slang for a pimp; literally 'mackerel' (the fish); the standard informal French term for a man who exploits and profits from sex workers.", "english_term": "pimp", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Etymology disputed — possibly from the fish's cold-blooded opportunism or from an older sense of 'go-between/intermediary'; Low German/Dutch borrowing also proposed. As common in informal French as 'pimp' is in English. Female form 'maquerelle' = brothel madam."}
